  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What to check first</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Licenses and third-party code</strong>Open-source and vendor code carries its own license terms.</li>
        <li><strong>Secrets</strong>Keys, tokens and credentials must be removed before any review.</li>
        <li><strong>Personal data in comments</strong>Reviews and issues can contain names and contact details.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What a review thread teaches a model</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A merged pull request is a decision with its reasoning attached. The code shows what changed; the review comments show why the first attempt was wrong, which is the part that generalizes to other codebases.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>The objection and the answer</strong>A reviewer rejecting an approach, and the change that satisfied the objection.</li>
        <li><strong>What testing caught</strong>The failure that appeared after the change, and the fix that followed it.</li>
        <li><strong>How a fix was narrowed</strong>A broad change cut back to the smallest thing that solved the problem.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What keeps a repository out of scope</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Code is the category where a licence, rather than a contract with a customer, usually decides the answer.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Licences that forbid sublicensing</strong>Copyleft and vendor terms can rule out sharing the code with a buyer, even in your own repository.</li>
        <li><strong>Secrets in commit history</strong>Keys, tokens and credentials have to be removed, and history has to be purged rather than overwritten.</li>
        <li><strong>Customer data in issues and comments</strong>Tickets, logs and review notes can carry names, addresses and account details.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Preparing a repository for review</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Code is the one category where most of the work happens before anyone looks at it, because history is the hard part. A repository that has never been cleaned is not ready to show a buyer.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Inventory the licences</strong>Open-source dependencies, vendored libraries and copied snippets each carry terms that decide whether the code can travel at all.</li>
        <li><strong>Purge secrets from history</strong>A key removed in the latest commit is still in the history. The history has to be rewritten, not overwritten.</li>
        <li><strong>Separate vendor and first-party code</strong>Reviewing your own work is straightforward; shipping somebody else&rsquo;s is a licence question.</li>
        <li><strong>Take customer data out of issues and comments</strong>Review threads are where the value sits, and they are also where names, addresses and account details end up.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

